================================================================================
Third-party trademarks (interoperability UI)
================================================================================

Harbor is a desktop client for driving and monitoring AI coding agents from
several vendors. To let a user identify which tool a given session or
provider option belongs to, the interface displays the following third-party
names and marks:

  - "Claude" / the Claude mark  (app/assets/logo-claude.svg)
    Claude and Anthropic are trademarks of Anthropic, PBC.

  - "OpenAI" / the OpenAI mark  (app/assets/logo-openai.svg)
    OpenAI is a trademark of OpenAI OpCo, LLC. Harbor also reuses this mark
    to label the "Codex" provider option, which runs OpenAI's Codex CLI.

  - "Cursor" / the Cursor mark  (app/assets/logo-cursor.svg)
    Cursor is a trademark of Anysphere, Inc.

These names and marks are the property of their respective owners. They are
used here solely to identify the corresponding third-party tool that a
given Harbor session or provider option is driving (nominative use), in the
same way a browser's address bar shows a site's own favicon. Their use here
does not imply any affiliation with, sponsorship by, or endorsement from
Anthropic, PBC, OpenAI OpCo, LLC, or Anysphere, Inc., and none is claimed.

Harbor could formerly drive a fourth third-party tool, herdr
(https://herdr.dev), as an OPTIONAL terminal/pane backend. That backend was
retired on 2026-08-14 and the integration was removed: Harbor's own session
daemon is the only backend and needs no separate install (see README.md and
docs/SESSION-DAEMON-CUTOVER.md). "herdr" survives in running text of this
repository (README, CLAUDE.md, docs/) only as history naming that former
optional dependency; no herdr logo or mark is bundled as an asset.

================================================================================
Unverified provenance
================================================================================

The following bundled files carry no license, attribution, or source
reference in this repository's history or on disk, so their exact origin
and licensing status could not be confirmed. They are listed here rather
than assigned a license, because doing so would mean inventing one.

  - app/assets/logo-claude.svg
    Unlike app/assets/logo-openai.svg and app/assets/logo-cursor.svg
    (which follow the standard export format of the Simple Icons project,
    https://simpleicons.org, whose SVG markup is released under CC0 1.0,
    separate from the trademark status of the marks they depict), this
    file does not match that or any other identifiable source format, and
    carries no attribution comment or reference. Where its path data was
    drawn from could not be confirmed from repository records. The
    trademark notice above applies to it regardless.

Recommendation before publishing: either establish and record a license
for this file, or replace/remove it.

A second entry used to sit here, app/assets/lighthouse-bg.jpg, a 3840x2160
photographic background of unverified provenance. It was referenced by no
code, so it has been DELETED rather than licensed, which is the other half
of the recommendation above. The README banner that now exists
(docs/hero.png) uses app/assets/hero-backdrop.jpg in its place: an abstract
texture with no subject, no place and no person in it, generated for this
project by an image model from the prompt recorded in
app/scripts/make-hero.js, which also composes the banner from that texture,
the app icon and the marks listed above. No third-party rights are claimed
in it and none are known.

Additionally, app/assets/fonts/ bundles two type families (IBM Plex Mono and
Schibsted Grotesk) with no OFL.txt, LICENSE, or other license file shipped
alongside them in this repository, and no such file exists anywhere else in
the repository either. These are widely known open-source families commonly
distributed under the SIL Open Font License, but that could not be confirmed
from this repository's own records, so it is noted here rather than asserted
as fact. THIS GAP IS STILL OPEN: bundling an OFL family requires shipping the
license text with it, so each family's own OFL.txt should be fetched from its
upstream project and committed next to the font files before publishing.

================================================================================
Third-party documentation mirror
================================================================================

This repository previously carried docs/upstream-herdr-docs/, a verbatim
mirror of documentation and Rust source files taken from the herdr project
(https://herdr.dev, https://github.com/ogulcancelik/herdr). herdr's own
README states it is licensed under AGPL-3.0, which this MIT-licensed
repository cannot redistribute on those terms, so that directory was
REMOVED rather than relicensed. No herdr material is bundled here now.

Harbor formerly drove herdr, when it was selected as the optional session
backend, as a separate program over its own documented interface. That
backend was retired on 2026-08-14 and the integration was removed.

